Falsifies `system_tests::user::authority_tests::the_root_supervisor_drops_its_construction_authority_and_cannot_build_again`.

Architecture: aarch64, riscv64, x86_64

The defect is the progenitor keeping the construction budget it is supposed to give away: one
line deleted from `root_supervisor`, the `cap_delete(ROOT_UT)` that drops the untyped itself.
Everything else runs unchanged, including the four wiring capabilities it still deletes and the
two proof attempts immediately below, which now succeed.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(dropped[1], 1, "progenitor still built a page or a kernel object
after deleting its untyped: the authority was not actually dropped")`. Confirmed red on
2026-09-16 at `system_tests/src/user/authority_tests.rs:165`.

**The kernel-side defect was tried first and is recorded here because it failed for the wrong
reason**, which is milestone 202's hazard and the thing this file exists to be honest about.
The claim is the kernel's, so the better-looking patch is `CapabilityTable::delete` taking a
non-consuming read instead of `Option::take`: `delete` returns `Ok(())`, the slot keeps its
capability, and the progenitor's retype succeeds. It does turn this test red. **It turns it red
at `authority_tests.rs:112`**, `assert_ne!(msg[0], REPORT_FAILED, "the supervision tree could
not be built: stage {}")`, inside the `run_tree` helper, because a `delete` that deletes
nothing breaks the construction of the supervision tree long before the progenitor gets to the
part where it drops anything. Right answer, useless diagnostic: nothing in "stage 3" says the
progenitor kept its authority.

So the fixture edit is the better falsification even though the kernel edit is the more
faithful defect, and the reason is stated rather than implied: a patch has to make **this
test's own assertion** fire, and a global break of `delete` fails an earlier assertion about
something else.

**What this record does not prove.** It proves the test notices when the drop does not happen.
It does not prove the kernel is what makes the drop stick, and the evidence for that lives
elsewhere: `capability::a_deleted_capability_stays_deleted` and
`capability::delete_touches_only_its_slot` are rows 4 and 5 of notes/confinement-claims.md and
both are `replayable` against the kernel-side defect this refused.

Replayed on all three ISAs. The hunk is portable code, so one patch serves every leg. On 2026-10-03 (UTC), `cargo xtask test --arch riscv64 --test the_root_supervisor_drops_its_construction_authority_and_cannot_build_again` and the same with `--arch x86_64` both came back red at `system_tests/src/user/authority_tests.rs:167`, the assertion this record predicts, which is where aarch64 was already red.

